// @check
// @error a method member `toString():string` may only appear in a BOUND — it demands that a call type-checks, which is not something a value carries. For a stored closure write the field form `name: (…) => R`; to constrain a type parameter write `<T: { toString():string }>`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `render` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `: string` / `: boolean` return annotations below are DECLARED rather than
// deleted: a fixture for a type feature should read as the documentation it is,
// and the checker calling them redundant is a fact about inference, not about
// the example. (`@hint` is how a corpus file says "yes, on purpose".)
// The INLINE twin of `error-bound-in-value-position.vl`. The parser accepts the
// method-member spelling in every type position on purpose, so that a bound reads
// the same wherever it is written; the grammar cannot see which position it is in,
// so the refusal lives in the checker.
//
// Both annotation ROUTES raise the same sentence — the rendered-name arm of
// `nameToTyReal` and the spelling-tree arm of `tsToTyReal` — because an annotation
// resolves through the tree when the parser recorded one and through the name when
// it did not, and a design rule enforced on one route only is a rule a re-render
// walks around.
//
// The remedy clause names BOTH real options rather than only the bound: an author
// who wanted a stored closure wrote the wrong one of two spellings, and those two
// spellings are a genuine pair (`{f: () => string}` = the data exists,
// `{f(): string}` = the call works).
function render(x: { toString(): string }): string { "x" }

print("unreached")
